WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.800
I study whiskers
because they're just the best.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.040
Most mammals, they have
whiskers, and what's exciting

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.360
about them is that there's so
much stuff that we don't know.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.240
I like to look at something
that we see every day

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.040
and then find out really cool

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.520
and interesting things
about them.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.720
Lots of people,
when they hear about whiskers,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.120
they think immediately
of cat whiskers.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.840
But, actually, other animals
have much better whiskers -

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.800
they're more sensitive,
they're bigger,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.120
they move
more than cats' whiskers.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.360
Porcupine whiskers are just
the longest whiskers I've seen.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.560
The one that I've got back
in the lab is about 45 cm long,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.400
and they move them
almost continually,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.000
and then they kind of bump
and move and vibrate

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.320
around over the material
that they're on

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.280
so you can really see that
they're moving all the time.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.440
Hi, gorgeous!

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.920
It's very difficult to study
the evolution of whiskers

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.280
because hair isn't really
preserved in the fossil record.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.280
So we have a look at
this little hole here

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.040
which is called the
infraorbital foramen,

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.280
or whisker holes.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.680
And all the information
from the whiskers,

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.440
from those sensitive follicles,

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.760
travel through that hole
and into the brain.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.080
Humans are really quite unusual
to not have whiskers.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.600
But we do still have
these whisker holes

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.120
where our whiskers
would have been

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.680
and, also, we even have
some remnants of muscles,

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.040
similar to what we see
in animals with whiskers.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.920
Whiskers are very much
like human fingertips.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.080
Lots of animals can move
their whiskers

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.200
and then some animals engage
in what is called whisking.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.200
So this is cyclic forward
and backward movements

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.800
that the animals make
with their whiskers.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.400
And we might think of this
as scanning.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.840
So, when we walk into a room,

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.080
we might be looking around
everywhere,

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.080
trying to see all around us,

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.720
and that's what these guys
are doing.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.520
And the fastest whisking
that I've seen

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.400
has been in harvest mice,

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.640
which reach up to
about 25 times per second,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.800
which are some of
the fastest movements

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.120
that mammals can make.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.000
The most sensitive whiskers
are in aquatic mammals.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.560
Lots of seals will have kind of
just under 2,000 nerve fibres

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.560
surrounding all of
those whiskers in the follicle

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.400
and their whiskers
are so sensitive

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.280
they can do this amazing thing

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.760
which is called
hydrodynamic sensing.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.640
So, as a fish swims through the
water, it leaves behind a wake,

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.840
a trail of water movement,

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.640
and the seals are able
to detect this.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.240
And they use
only their whiskers for this.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.400
These are porcupine whiskers

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.680
and you can see

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.720
that they're arranged into
a grid, or whisker map.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.800
So you have rows and columns
of whiskers.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.360
So they're very ordered.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.160
The same grid-like pattern
can be seen

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.120
in physical structures
through the brain,

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.200
and now neuroscientists
love this because it means

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.760
they can actually
tweak one whisker here,

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.080
so a middle whisker,
and they can follow it

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.480
through the entire brain

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.600
to see where
that sensory signal goes,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.400
and each physical structure
will light up in turn.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:36.880
In many animals,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:41.320
whiskers are their primary
and most important sense.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.040
So it's very, very important
not to trim them.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.360
It wouldn't hurt them,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.240
but they'll suddenly
remove a sense.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.000
So it would be like
if you blindfolded us

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:52.280
and then put us in a room,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.240
and so we've got to feel around
to work out where we are,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.480
and that is what these animals
are doing all the time.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.360
We can see that,
when we look at rats and mice,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.080
that some of them will actually

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.360
engage in a behaviour
called barbering.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:07.920
So this is when you have

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.400
a dominant individual
that will trim the whiskers,

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.680
so bite off the whiskers
of their family

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.960
or other people
that live in their box.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.240
And so, when they do that,

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.720
those individuals will become
more submissive.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.040
So it establishes
this hierarchy within the cage.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.720
Whiskers can inspire lots of
new technology and innovations.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.240
Firstly, we can have a look
at their shape.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.600
So the undulations
of seal whiskers has inspired,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.600
for instance, turbine blades.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:43.440
So turbine blades
can be extra-aerodynamic

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.040
because they have these amazing

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:46.760
undulations or waves
along them.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.360
These could also be applied
to tidal energy, as well.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.880
Then you have the fact
that they are sensors.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.720
You can put these sensors
onto robots.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.800
So, then, you can have
tactile robots or whisker bots.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.960
And these could be
really useful

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.920
for something even like
a robot hoover.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.360
But also to make sure
that robots can go

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.720
into hazardous, dark
or complex environments.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.840
These are the environments
we need our robots to go into.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.160
So people don't really think
about whiskers at all.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.400
You probably go home
and look at your cat

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.080
or your gerbil or your rabbit
and you think, "Oh, yeah,

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:23.560
"they're fluffy
and have whiskers."

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.040
But, actually, what we're doing
is trying to understand,

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.120
"Well, how do they work,
and how sensitive are they,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.360
"and what do they
use them for?"

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.920
And I think that that's
super interesting to find out.

